  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSfc ridge of high pressure has maintained a rather quiet and mild \nday across the forecast area. Light and variable winds with early \nafternoon temperature readings in the mid 60s to low 70s have been \ncommon. Mid to high level clouds have been tough to depart our far \nnortheast zones, mainly across far northeast SD and west central MN \nwhere temps have remained the coolest.\n\nThe sfc high will continue to drift east-southeast into MN and WI \ntonight as sfc low pressure and warm front trek east across NE. A \nfew CAMs are still hinting at warm advection elevated convection \ndeveloping late this afternoon into this evening across parts of \nsouth central and southeast SD. RRFS/RAP/NAM soundings across south \ncentral SD show an increase in moisture at about 800mb and steeper \nlapse rates aloft. If anything were to get going, it likely won't \nimpact our forecast area as southeast SD into northwest IA remain in \na more favorable spot.\n\nOn Friday, the aforementioned warm front shifts into central SD by \nthe end of the day as sfc low pressure strengthens and becomes more \norganized across the Northern High Plains. A tightening pressure \ngradient will lead to a more breezy to windy day with southeast \nwinds gusting to around 20-25 mph across our eastern zones while our \nwest will see gusts closer to 30-35 mph. This will help pull in a \nwarmer, more moist air mass with dew point temperatures expected to \nincrease into the 50s to perhaps low 60s. Even though daytime highs \nreach the 70s to low 80s, fire weather conditions should remain \nmuted due to the more humid air mass in place.\n\nThis won't be the case on Saturday as sfc low pressure tracks into \nthe western Dakotas and pushes that warm front through the area. \nSouth to southwest winds will increase through the day. Highest \nwinds will peak across our eastern zones with gusts ranging between \n30-45 mph with higher gusts possible across the downslope areas of \nthe Prairie Coteau in northeast SD. Temperatures will top out rather \nwarm to hot Saturday afternoon with readings in the 80s, which is \nabout 20-25 degrees above normal. Some spots west river in south \ncentral SD could hit 90 degrees. These readings will be mostly shy \nof record highs with the exception of Watertown where 86 is the \ncurrent record. There will be a better chance of seeing record warm \novernight lows Saturday morning with lows in the low to mid 60s. \nFire weather will become a concern on Saturday as afternoon RH \nvalues drop into a 20-30 percent range. With strongest winds \nexpected across our eastern zones, the grassland fire danger index \nwill top out in the high to potentially very high category Saturday \nafternoon.\n\nChanges in this warm and dry pattern return as early as the latter \nhalf of the weekend as sfc low pressure and a cold front encroach on \nthe area Sunday. Winds will become northerly on Sunday as the front \npasses through the area. Increasing chances for moisture will appear \nby late in the day across north central SD. Better chances for more \nwidespread rainfall moves into the area overnight into Monday with \nbasically the northern 2/3 of the forecast area having a 30-60 \npercent chance of rain. Temperatures will turn much cooler by early \nnext week with daytime highs not expected to get out of the 50s to \naround 60 degrees.",
